# Highlighting sampler

Two roots in two languages. Each root pulls an abstract chunk in through a
hole, so the weaver colors both grammars *and* splices a chunk-use back
inside the coloring (SPEC.md §5.5). The language is resolved out of band from
each root's file extension — no per-chunk annotation.

<<greeting>>=
"hello from a literate program"
@

<<src/demo.rs>>=
fn main() {
    let msg = <<greeting>>;
    println!("{msg}");
}
@

The Nix side is a tiny flake, so lit can eventually present its own `flake.nix`
as a literate document and self-host.

<<packages>>=
[ pkgs.hello pkgs.ripgrep ]
@

<<flake.nix>>=
{
  description = "a tiny flake";
  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: {
    packages.x86_64-linux.default = <<packages>>;
  };
}
@
